# Phase 20 — System Design

> Distributed systems fundamentals, filtered for what AI workloads actually stress. The patterns are classical; what's different is the scale of the numbers — a 4-second dependency you don't control, costing money per call.

**Scope note.** Several topics here are covered in depth in **`BackendForAi.md`** — caching (Ch 17), rate limiting (Ch 2/4), idempotency (Ch 1/11), retries and circuit breakers (Ch 8), queues and DLQs (Ch 11), observability (Ch 21). This phase gives the system-design framing and the AI-specific pressure points, and points there for implementation.

---

## 1. What AI Workloads Change

```
 CLASSIC WEB SERVICE              AI SERVICE
 ──────────────────               ──────────
 requests: 50 ms                  requests: 4,000 ms          ← 80×
 cost/request: ~$0.000001         cost/request: ~$0.001–0.50  ← 1000×+
 stateless and cheap to retry     retries cost real money
 CPU-bound at scale               waiting-bound; GPU-bound upstream
 failures are rare                upstream 429/5xx are ROUTINE
 responses are cacheable          responses are non-deterministic
 capacity = requests/second       capacity = CONCURRENT CONNECTIONS

 CONSEQUENCE: patterns that are optional in a normal backend —
 circuit breakers, idempotency, backpressure, budget enforcement —
 are MANDATORY here.
```

---

## 2. Monolith vs Microservices

```
 START WITH A MODULAR MONOLITH. Almost always.

 ✔ One deploy, one database, one trace, one place to debug
 ✔ Refactoring across boundaries is a rename, not a migration
 ✔ No network calls between your own components
 ✘ Everything scales together
 ✘ One bad deploy affects everything

 SPLIT WHEN THERE IS A CONCRETE REASON:
   • different SCALING profiles      ← the real reason in AI systems
   • different HARDWARE (GPU vs CPU) ← the other real reason
   • different LANGUAGES
   • team ownership boundaries at ~50+ engineers
```

**The AI-specific split that's always correct:**

```
   API tier          stateless, CPU, scales on request concurrency, 2 s start
   Inference tier    GPU, scales on queue depth, 3 min start
   Worker tier       CPU/GPU, scales on queue latency, long-running jobs

 These have genuinely different scaling reasons and hardware.
 Splitting them is not premature — it's the first correct decomposition.
```

**Design the monolith so it can split.** Group routers by domain, keep URL prefixes aligned with those domains, and enforce layering (api → service → repository). Then extracting a domain is a load-balancer rule, not a client migration.

