## 3. API Gateway

```
 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ GATEWAY DOES                     │ APPLICATION DOES                  │
 ├──────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
 │ TLS termination                  │ per-user / per-tenant rate limits  │
 │ IP-based rate limiting           │ token QUOTAS and spend budgets     │
 │ Request size limits              │ object-level authorization         │
 │ Coarse auth (is the JWT valid?)  │ business validation                │
 │ Routing to services              │ model routing and fallback         │
 │ Response caching                 │ semantic caching                   │
 └──────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┘

 THE RULE: if it needs application knowledge (tenants, models, tokens),
 it belongs in the app. Otherwise push it to the gateway — cheaper,
 faster, and already battle-tested.

 NEVER push object-level authorization to the gateway. It doesn't know
 your domain model, and that is where breaches come from.
```

**AI-specific gateway note:** an LLM gateway (LiteLLM proxy, Portkey, or your own) is a *second* gateway, in front of providers rather than in front of your API. It handles virtual keys, per-team budgets, provider fallback, and spend tracking. Worth adopting early — those are things you'd otherwise build twice.

