## 6. Retries, Circuit Breakers, and Backpressure

*(Implementation: `BackendForAi.md` Ch 8.)*

```
 RETRY RULES
   ✔ Retry at exactly ONE layer. Client + gateway + app each retrying 3×
     is 27 requests to a struggling service from one user action.
   ✔ Exponential backoff WITH FULL JITTER: delay = uniform(0, min(base·2ⁿ, cap))
     Without jitter, every failed client retries at the same instant.
   ✔ Cap TOTAL retry time, not just attempt count — 3 attempts with backoff
     can consume 30 s of a 10 s request budget, making the outage worse.
   ✘ NEVER retry a 4xx across providers — the request itself is bad and every
     provider rejects it identically. You'd pay 3× for the same failure.
   ⚠ A timed-out LLM call may have SUCCEEDED and charged you. Use an
     idempotency key with the provider where supported.
```

```
 CIRCUIT BREAKER — why it matters MORE for AI

   When a provider has an incident, every request takes the full 120 s
   timeout before failing. With 200 concurrent requests, your workers are
   saturated with doomed calls for two minutes and HEALTHY endpoints stop
   responding.

   CLOSED ──(N failures)──► OPEN ──(after timeout)──► HALF-OPEN
                             │                            │
                             fail fast in ~1 µs      one probe: success→CLOSED
                                                            failure→OPEN

   A breaker turns a 120,000 ms failure into a 0.001 ms one.
   One per provider, with a metric and an alert.
```

```
 BACKPRESSURE
   When you cannot keep up, REJECT rather than queue unboundedly.
   ✔ Bounded queues; 429 with Retry-After when full
   ✔ Semaphores on every fan-out — unbounded gather() exhausts pools and
     trips provider rate limits (which is SLOWER than bounded concurrency)
   ✔ Admission control: reject at the edge, don't accept work you can't do
   ✘ An unbounded queue converts an overload into a total outage with
     a very long tail
```

