## 8. Rate Limiting

```
 FOUR LAYERS, EACH ANSWERING A DIFFERENT QUESTION

 ① IP rate limit          "is this unauthenticated abuse?"     gateway
 ② Per-key requests/min   "is this client too chatty?"          app
 ③ Per-org token quota    "have they used their allowance?"     app
 ④ Global spend circuit   "is something catastrophically wrong?" app

 ④ IS THE ONE THAT SAVES YOU. Per-org limits assume the bug is in one
   org's usage. A prompt loop, retry storm, or leaked key affects
   everything at once — only a global ceiling stops it.
```

```
 ALGORITHMS
   Fixed window     simple; allows a 2× burst at the boundary
   Sliding window   accurate; a Redis sorted set (ZREMRANGEBYSCORE + ZCARD)
   Token bucket     allows bursts up to capacity; the best default
   Leaky bucket     smooths output; used for outbound provider limiting

 ⚠ IN-MEMORY RATE LIMITING IS WRONG with multiple workers or pods —
   your effective limit is multiplied by the process count, and it
   resets on every deploy. Use Redis.

 ⚠ RATE-LIMIT YOUR OWN OUTBOUND CALLS TOO. Providers enforce both
   requests/minute AND tokens/minute. Exceeding either costs latency.
```

