## 9. Scaling, Load Balancing, and Replication

```
 VERTICAL          bigger machine. Simple, has a ceiling, single point of failure.
 HORIZONTAL        more machines. Requires statelessness. The default.

 STATELESSNESS IS THE PREREQUISITE for horizontal scaling.
 In AI systems the usual violation is a model loaded in-process (Phase 19).
```

```
 LOAD BALANCING
   Round-robin        fine for short requests
   LEAST-CONNECTIONS  ← CORRECT for long-lived connections and streams;
                        round-robin distributes connections evenly at creation
                        but they accumulate unevenly
   Consistent hashing when you need cache/session affinity
   ⚠ Enable HTTP/2 — browsers cap HTTP/1.1 at ~6 connections per origin,
     so a user with 7 tabs of your streaming app gets a silent hang.
```

```
 DATABASE REPLICATION
   Primary + read replicas: writes to one, reads spread across many.
   ⚠ REPLICATION LAG (usually ms, occasionally seconds) means read-after-write
     can return stale data. Route reads that must be fresh to the primary.

 AI-SPECIFIC READ SPLIT
   Vector search is read-heavy and CPU-intensive. Once volume is real,
   route it to a replica so it doesn't compete with OLTP traffic.
```

**The connection-pool wall everyone hits:**
```
 pool_size × workers × pods  vs  Postgres max_connections (default 100)
   10 × 4 × 20 = 800  →  "FATAL: sorry, too many clients already"

 FIX: shrink the pool (async holds connections only during a query, not
 during the 4 s LLM call), or PgBouncer in transaction mode.
```

