## 12. High Availability & Fault Tolerance

```
 REMOVE SINGLE POINTS OF FAILURE
   ✔ ≥3 API pods across ≥2 availability zones; PodDisruptionBudget
   ✔ Database with automated failover (managed service, or Patroni)
   ✔ Redis Sentinel or Cluster
   ✔ MULTIPLE LLM PROVIDERS ← the AI-specific one. A single provider is a
     single point of failure you do not control and cannot fix.

 GRACEFUL DEGRADATION — decide BEFORE the incident, per dependency
   ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ Redis (cache)    │ serve uncached — slower, correct                 │
   │ Redis (limits)   │ fail OPEN with an alarm + a cheap in-process cap │
   │ Vector store     │ answer without retrieval, LABELLED; or 503 for   │
   │                  │ RAG endpoints only                               │
   │ LLM provider     │ fall back → cheaper model → retrieval-only → 503 │
   │ Database         │ 503. There is no degraded mode for the source    │
   │                  │ of truth.                                        │
   │ Object storage   │ queue uploads; reads fail with 503               │
   └──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

 THE DEGRADATION LADDER FOR GENERATION
   primary model → secondary provider (different outage domain)
   → cheaper model → RETRIEVAL-ONLY with a clear label → 503

 That "retrieval-only" tier is often the difference between a bad hour
 and an outage. Users can still find their answer in the passages.
```

**Timeout budgets must decrease inward:**
```
 client 180 s > LB 150 s > app 120 s > LLM call 110 s > connect 5 s
 If the LB times out first, the client gets a generic 504 and your app
 learns nothing about what actually failed.
```

