## 13. Observability

*(Implementation: Phase 19 and `BackendForAi.md` Ch 21.)* The system-design point:

```
 MONITORING answers questions you knew to ask (dashboards, alerts).
 OBSERVABILITY answers questions you didn't (high-cardinality traces).

 You need both. In a distributed AI system, the question you didn't know
 to ask is usually "why was THIS request slow/wrong/expensive?" —
 and only a trace with the request ID answers it.
```

```
 THE MINIMUM VIABLE SET
   ✔ A request ID generated at the edge, propagated everywhere, returned
     to the client, and present on every log line
   ✔ Structured JSON logs to stdout with tenant, model, tokens, and COST
   ✔ RED metrics + TTFT + cost + saturation (loop lag, pools, queues)
   ✔ Traces at 10% sampling, 100% on errors
   ✔ Alerts on symptoms users feel, each with a runbook

 Without the request ID, "it was wrong yesterday at 14:32" is unactionable.
 With it, it's one query.
```

