## Common Mistakes

```
❌ Microservices before there's a scaling or hardware reason
❌ Kafka for a workload doing thousands of events per day
❌ Non-idempotent tasks with at-least-once delivery
❌ Enqueue before commit (or commit then enqueue) without an outbox
❌ Retries at every layer → 27× amplification onto a struggling service
❌ Retry without jitter → synchronised thundering herd on recovery
❌ Falling back to another provider on a 4xx
❌ No circuit breaker → a provider incident saturates your workers
❌ Unbounded queues → overload becomes a long-tailed total outage
❌ Unbounded fan-out (gather over 10,000 items) → pool exhaustion and 429s
❌ In-memory rate limiting with multiple workers
❌ No global spend ceiling — only per-org limits
❌ Cache keys without a tenant ID
❌ Round-robin load balancing for long-lived streams
❌ pool_size × workers × pods > max_connections
❌ Redis lock released with DEL instead of a compare-and-delete
❌ Trying to fix async-ingestion "staleness" with stronger consistency
   instead of showing job status
❌ Single LLM provider — a SPOF you don't control
❌ No decided degradation behaviour per dependency
```

